"At the beginning of Surah al-Waqi'ah, the event of the Resurrection is described. The word 'al-Waqi'ah' means an event that is bound to happen. When the Day of Resurrection occurs, no one will be left to deny it, for it is an event so immense that it will compel everyone into helpless affirmation. This event humbles some and exalts others; the earth is shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains are crumbled into dust. Then people are divided into three groups: the Companions of the Right (ashab al-maymanah), the Companions of the Left (ashab al-mash'amah), and the Foremost (al-sabiqun). The Companions of the Right are those endowed with auspiciousness and blessing, whereas the Companions of the Left are wretched and worthless individuals. The Foremost are those who have preceded others in good deeds and are the ones brought near (al-muqarrabun) to Allah.\n\nFor the Foremost (al-sabiqun), there are in Paradise thrones encrusted with jewels, youthful attendants, wine that neither causes headaches nor intoxicates, varied fruits, and fair houris. Most of them are from the earlier communities, and a few from later generations. The blessings of this group are described by likening them to the highest pleasures of this world. In Paradise, they hear no vain talk, only words of peace (salam). These blessings are a reward for their deeds.\n\nThe Companions of the Right (ashab al-yamin) are the felicitous ones who follow the Foremost and are given their records of deeds in their right hands. The description of their Paradise is depicted in a manner that would particularly appeal to bedouins: thornless lote-trees (sidr), clustered bananas, extended shade, pouring waters, and unfailing fruits. For them, there are raised couches and houris created anew, formed by restoring elderly women to youth. These houris are of equal age, loving, and beautiful women. These blessings of the Companions of the Right are entirely by the grace of Allah; for regarding this group, the expression 'as a reward for what they did' is not mentioned.\n\nThe Companions of the Left (ashab al-shimal) are the wretched who are given their records in their left hands. Their punishment consists of a scorching fire, boiling water, and a shadow of black smoke that is neither cool nor beneficial. The reason for this is that while in the world, they indulged in luxury, committed sins, and persisted in the great sin (shirk or false oaths). They used to deny the Resurrection. In the Hereafter, they will eat from the tree of Zaqqum, fill their bellies with it, and drink boiling water on top of it, drinking insatiably like thirst-stricken camels. This will be their hospitality on the Day of Recompense.\n\nIn the continuation of the surah, attention is drawn to the power of Allah against the disbelievers' denial of the Resurrection: the creation of man (from semen), the bringing forth of vegetation from the soil, the sending down of water from the clouds, and the creation of fire are cited as examples. These proofs demonstrate that those who know the initial creation ought to accept the Resurrection as well. Had Allah willed, He could have rendered the semen futile, turned the crops into dry chaff, made the water salty, and extinguished the fire. Therefore, human beings must show gratitude and exalt the name of their Lord, the Magnificent (al-Azim). These verses emphasize human helplessness and Allah's absolute power.\n\nThe expression 'that We may change your forms' mentioned in verse 61 has been interpreted differently by commentators. The word 'amthal' can be taken either as the plural of 'mithl' (like/similar) or as the plural of 'mathal' (attribute, form). In the first case, the meaning is: 'We have decreed death among you so that We may bring your likes.' In the second case: 'We are able to eliminate you and create you in another form.' Hasan al-Basri interpreted this verse as pointing to a worldly transformation, taking it as a threat of being transformed into apes and swine. The verse encompasses both worldly transformation and resurrection in the Hereafter.\n\nIn verses 62–63, human beings are urged to reflect on their first creation. Man, who was created from dust stage by stage, should realize that the same Power is capable of resurrecting him. Then the subject turns to provision, and attention is drawn to the seed that is sown: 'Have you seen the seed that you sow? Is it you who cause it to grow, or are We the Grower?' According to al-Qurtubi, it is commendable (mustahabb) for anyone sowing a crop to recite this verse and state that it is Allah who makes it grow.\n\nIn verses 64–66, it is emphasized that it is Allah who causes the seed to grow. The word 'hutam' means dry debris; when the crop is broken down and turned into dry chaff, people say in bewilderment, 'We are ruined, we are undone.' This is an expression of ingratitude and despair.\n\nIn verses 68–70, attention is drawn to the water that is drunk. It is reminded that Allah is able to make the water sent down from the rainclouds (muzn) salty and bitter (ujaj). While gratitude should be shown for these blessings, people immediately fall into despair at the moment of deprivation.\n\nIn verses 71–74, fire and its tree are addressed. It is Allah who created the tree of fire; this fire is both a reminder and a means of utility for travelers. In conclusion, one is commanded to glorify their Lord, who bestows all these blessings, by His name 'al-Azim' (the Magnificent).\n\nIn verses 75–82, an oath is sworn by the positions of the stars. It has been debated whether the particle 'la' is redundant (za'id), emphatic (ta'kid), or negative, and preferably it has been given a negative meaning. The Quran is an honorable and well-preserved Book; none can touch it except the purified. The disbelievers belittle this discourse and make their livelihood consist solely of denial.\n\nIn verses 83–87, the moment of death is depicted. When the soul reaches the throat, people look on helplessly. Yet Allah is nearer to the dying person than they are. If they are truthful in their claims, let them bring the soul back! This demonstrates their utter helplessness.\n\nIn verses 88–96, the ultimate end of the three groups is explained: for those brought near (al-muqarrabun), there is rest, bountiful provision, and a Garden of Delight (Jannat al-Na'im). The Companions of the Right greet one another with peace. For the deniers who went astray, there is boiling water and Hellfire. All of this is absolute truth (haqq al-yaqin), that is, certain reality. Finally, it is once again commanded to glorify one's Lord by His name 'al-Azim'."